# Line endings for files Windows interprets itself (D17, new-PC install review 2026-09-24).
#
# cmd.exe finds a `goto`/`call` label by re-reading the batch file, and with LF-only line
# endings that lookup is known to misbehave -- labels are missed or the jump lands in the wrong
# place, depending on where 512-byte boundaries fall. Every .bat here uses labels (quickstart.bat
# and setup.bat depend on them). Until now there was no .gitattributes: the index holds .bat as
# LF and a checkout got CRLF only from a machine-wide `core.autocrlf=true`. A clone with
# autocrlf=false, and GitHub's "Download ZIP" (which the README recommends), therefore delivered
# LF-only batch files. `eol=crlf` makes every checkout AND every `git archive` (the release ZIP,
# GitHub's ZIP) write CRLF, whatever the machine's autocrlf says. The index keeps LF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Keep source and documentation stable across Windows and Unix checkouts.
# PowerShell accepts LF; UTF-8 BOMs needed by Windows PowerShell are preserved.
*.py text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=lf

# .vbs retains its existing autocrlf-dependent checkout behavior.

# The work ledger is append-only: every PR adds a dated entry at the END, so concurrent
# PRs always touch the same last lines. merge=union keeps both sides' added lines instead of
# reporting a conflict. Local merges only: GitHub's server-side merge ignores this attribute
# (see docs/private/ledger.d/README.md for the per-entry-file route that works there too).
docs/private/claude_work_ledger.md merge=union
